abstract: Xi yi xin fa [New methods of Western medicine] is a late-Qing Chinese medical
  work concerning surgery and bandaging, credited to John Glasgow Kerr (嘉約翰) for oral
  translation and Lin Xiangdong (林湘東) for written rendering. The official fixed catalogue
  for Bodleian Libraries, Sinica 1826, supplies [Canton] and [1872]. Derived from
  the Bodleian digital images, this dataset provides an AI-assisted transcription
  of original printed text across six figure faces, thirty-seven main-text faces,
  and one face with an original printed frame and a blank body. TXT, JSON, and TEI
  XML retain readings, uncertainties, and physical-face-to-image mappings for research
  on late-Qing surgical and bandaging terminology in translation, image-text relationships,
  and print history. This AI-assisted revised edition follows comparison with second
  image observations and source review; uncertainty and unresolved cases remain. Full
  human double collation and expert review have not been performed.
